text:
pkgdown can render Quarto vignettes, but it replaces Quarto’s styling with its own (theme: "none", minimal: TRUE). As a result, Quarto features such as callout blocks, code annotations and tabsets don’t render correctly; callouts, for instance, become plain blockquotes.
quartodown renders the whole site with Quarto itself, so you get:

full Quarto support, on every page;
a package website layout: a home page sidebar, a grouped function reference, articles and a changelog;
function links with hover tooltips.

Details
build_site() assembles a Quarto website project in _quarto/ from the package sources and _quartodown.yml, then renders it:


the function reference: a grouped index, and a page per man/*.Rd file with its examples run, except \keyword{internal} ones not listed under reference:;


the vignettes, including those in vignettes/articles/;


NEWS.md as the changelog, with issues (#123) and GitHub users (@user) linked;


LICENSE.md, the LICENSE file, and CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md, CONTRIBUTING.md and SUPPORT.md (at the top level or in .github/);


the navbar, with a "Get started" link, the function reference, an "Articles" menu and the changelog;


the home page, built from README.md plus a sidebar (links, license, citation, developers and badges);


code output printed in the code’s block, each line prefixed with #&gt; (the copy button leaves those lines out);


function calls in R code, and inline code like mean(), linked to their documentation with downlit;


the "Authors and Citation" page.
